  </p>

  <p>
    We have similar definitions for scalar-valued functions.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Local Minimums and Maximums</title>

    <statement>
      <p>Given a function <m>f(x, y)</m> :</p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f(x, y)</m> has a local maximum at <m>(a, b)</m> if
              <m>f(a, b) \geq f(x, y)</m> for all points <m>(x, y)</m>
              inside some open circle of radius <m>r \gt 0</m> centered at
              <m>(a, b)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f(x, y)</m> has a local minimum at <m>(a, b)</m> if
              <m>f(a, b) \leq f(x, y)</m> for all points <m>(x, y)</m>
              inside some open circle of radius <m>r \gt 0</m> centered at
              <m>(a, b)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      This definition says that we must compare <m>f(a, b)</m> to all
      points inside some open circle in the domain of <m>f</m>. Moreover,
      we only need to find one such circle that works.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-06-open-circle">
      <caption>An open circle around a point.</caption>

