    The graphs above show only the plots; you can drag and zoom them. To see the expressions behind them, or to save a copy and create your own version of the graphs, open them in a new page from here:
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the left graph</url>,
    <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the right graph</url>
  </p>

  <note>
    <p>
      In Calculus I, when working with functions of one variable
      <m>y=f(x)</m>, we used critical points to find local
      maximums/minimums (places where the derivative is zero or doesn't
      exist). We will do the same thing with functions of two or more
      variables.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Critical Points</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a function <m>f(x, y)</m>, we say that the interior point
        <m>(a, b)</m> of the domain of <m>f</m> is a critical point of
        <m>f</m> if one of the following is true:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p><m>f_{x}(a, b)=0</m> and <m>f_{y}(a, b)=0</m>.</p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              At least one of <m>f_{x}(a, b)</m> or <m>f_{y}(a, b)</m> does
              not exist.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m> (a, b ) </m>is a critical point of <m>f</m> due to
            condition 1 , this implies the tangent plane to <m>f</m> at
            <m>(a, b, f(a, b))</m> is horizontal.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Condition 2) is for the points that the function is not
            differentiable at.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If a function <m>f(x, y)</m> has a critical point at
            <m>(a, b)</m>, this does not guarantee that <m>f</m> has a local
            maximum/minimum at <m>(a, b)</m>. However, if <m>f</m> does have
            a local maximum/minimum at some point, it must occur at a
            critical point.
          </p>
        </li>
        <!--\item Critical points must be interior points of the domain of $f$.-->
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Interior and Boundary Points</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a set <m>S</m> in the <m>x y</m>-plane, we say that the point
        ( <m>a, b</m> ) is an interior point of <m>S</m> if there exists an
        open circle of radius <m>r \gt 0</m> centered at ( <m>a, b</m> ) that
        is entirely inside <m>S</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Given a set <m>S</m> in the <m>x y</m>-plane, we say that the point
        <m>(a, b)</m> is a boundary point of <m>S</m> if every open circle
        of radius <m>r \gt 0</m> centered at ( <m>a, b</m> ) lies partly
        inside and partly outside of <m>S</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-06-interior-boundary">
    <caption>A region with boundary and interior points.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_20_48d64282dad3782ea2aeg-4-1. -->
