    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <note>
    <p>
      If the domain of a function <m>f(x, y)</m> is <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m>,
      then all points in its domain are interior points.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-06-ex-12-2">
    <statement>
      <p>Find all critical points of the following functions:</p>

      <p>(a) <m>f(x, y)=x^{3}+3 x^{2}y+ 3xy^2+y^3</m>.</p>

      <p>(b) <m>f(x, y)=e^{y}-y e^{x}</m>.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Both partial derivatives exist at every point of
        <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, so the critical points come from solving
        <m>f_x=0</m> and <m>f_y=0</m> simultaneously. Computing the partial
        derivatives:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>f_x(x,y) = 3x^2 + 6xy + 3y^2 = 3(x+y)^2</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y(x,y) = 3x^2 + 6xy + 3y^2 = 3(x+y)^2</mrow>
        </md>
        The two partial derivatives are identical, and
        <m>3(x+y)^2 = 0 \Rightarrow x+y = 0 \Rightarrow y=-x</m>. Hence
        every point on the line <m>y=-x</m> is a critical point of <m>f</m>;
        that is, the critical points are all the points of the form
        <m>(a,-a)</m> with <m>a</m> any real number. (Notice that
        <m>f(x,y)=(x+y)^3</m>, which explains why an entire line of critical
        points appears.)
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Both partial derivatives exist at every point of
        <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>f_x(x,y) = -y e^{x}</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y(x,y) = e^{y} - e^{x}</mrow>
        </md>
        Since <m>e^{x} \gt 0</m> for every <m>x</m>, the equation
        <m>f_x = 0</m> forces <m>y=0</m>. Substituting <m>y=0</m> into
        <m>f_y=0</m> gives
        <m>e^{0}-e^{x} = 0 \Rightarrow e^{x} = 1 \Rightarrow x = 0</m>.
        Hence the only critical point of <m>f</m> is <m>(0,0)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-06-ex-12-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x, y)=x^2+y^2</m>. This function has a
        critical point at <m>(0,0)</m>. Does <m>(0,0)</m> correspond to a
        local max, a local min, or neither?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We have <m>f(0,0)=0</m>, and since squares are never negative,
        <m>f(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \geq 0 = f(0,0)</m> for every point <m>(x,y)</m>
        in the plane. In particular <m>f(0,0)\leq f(x,y)</m> for all points
        inside any open circle of radius <m>r \gt 0</m> centered at
        <m>(0,0)</m>, and by the definition of local minimums, <m>f</m> has
        a local minimum at <m>(0,0)</m>. In fact, since the inequality holds
        for every point of the plane and not just near the origin,
        <m>(0,0)</m> is a global minimum: the graph of <m>f</m> is a
        paraboloid opening upwards with its vertex at the origin.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Three Classifications for Critical Points</title>

    <p>
      Given a function <m>f(x, y)</m> and a critical point ( <m>a, b</m> )
      of <m>f</m>, only one of three things can occur:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p><m>f</m> has a local max at <m>(a, b)</m>.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>f</m> has a local min at <m>(a, b)</m>.</p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p><m>f</m> has a saddle point at <m>(a, b)</m>.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Saddle Points</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a function <m>f(x, y), f</m> has a saddle point at
        ( <m>a, b</m> ) if for all open circles of radius <m>r \gt 0</m>
        centered at ( <m>a, b</m> ), we can always find points
        <m>\left(x_{0}, y_{0}\right)</m> and <m>\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)</m>
        in the circle such that <m>f(a, b) \lt f\left(x_{0}, y_{0}\right)</m>
        and <m>f(a, b) \gt f\left(x_{1}, y_{1}\right)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-06-ex-12-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using the picture below, explain why the critical point
        <m>(0,0)</m> of <m>f(x, y)=2x^2-3y^2</m> is a saddle point.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch03-06-ex-12-4">
        <caption>The surface z equals f of x and y, over a closed bounded region, showing the location of saddle point. The surface increases if one moves along the direction of the x-axis and decreases if one moves along the direction of the y-axis.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_09_20_48d64282dad3782ea2aeg-8. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch03-06-ex-12-4" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_09_20_48d64282dad3782ea2aeg-8.png">
              <shortdescription>The surface z equals f of x and y, over a closed bounded region, showing the location of saddle point. The surface increases if one moves along the direction of the x-axis and decreases if one moves along the direction of the y-axis.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The surface <m>z=f(x, y)</m>, over a closed bounded region, showing the location of saddle point. The surface increases if one moves along the direction of the <m>x</m>-axis and decreases if one moves along the direction of the <m>y</m>-axis.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Graph of <m>2x^2-3y^2</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We have <m>f(0,0)=0</m>. Consider any open circle of radius
        <m>r \gt 0</m> centered at <m>(0,0)</m>. Moving away from the origin
        along the <m>x</m>-axis (<m>y=0</m>), this is the direction in the
        picture along which the surface increases. Moving away from the
        origin along the <m>y</m>-axis (<m>x=0</m>), this is the direction
        in the picture along which the surface decreases. Since every open
        circle centered at <m>(0,0)</m> contains points where <m>f</m> is
        larger than <m>f(0,0)</m> and points where <m>f</m> is smaller than
        <m>f(0,0)</m>, the critical point <m>(0,0)</m> is neither a maximum
        nor a minimum.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    Given a critical point <m>(a, b)</m>, there is an algebraic way to
    determine whether it corresponds to a local min, local max, or a saddle
    point. Unfortunately, this method is not perfect.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Second Derivative Test</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose you have a function <m>f(x, y)</m> and a critical point
        <m>(a, b)</m> of <m>f</m>. Let
        <md>
          D(x, y)=f_{x x} f_{y y}-f_{x y}^{2}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>Then:</p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f</m> has a local maximum at <m>(a, b)</m> if
              <m>f_{x x}(a, b) \lt 0</m> and <m>D(a, b) \gt 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f</m> has a local minimum at <m>(a, b)</m> if
              <m>f_{x x}(a, b) \gt 0</m> and <m>D(a, b) \gt 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f</m> has a saddle point at <m>(a, b)</m> if
              <m>D(a, b) \lt 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The test is inconclusive at <m>(a, b)</m> if <m>D(a, b)=0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        We call the function <m>D(x, y)</m> the discriminant of <m>f</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch03-06-ex-12-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find and classify the critical points of
        <m>f(x, y)=x^3+3x^2y+3xy^2+y^3+3y^2+6y-3x</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Both partial derivatives exist at every point of
        <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>, so we solve <m>f_x=0</m> and <m>f_y=0</m>
        simultaneously. Computing the partial derivatives:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>f_x(x,y) = 3x^2 + 6xy + 3y^2 - 3 = 3(x+y)^2 - 3</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y(x,y) = 3x^2 + 6xy + 3y^2 + 6y + 6 = 3(x+y)^2 + 6y + 6</mrow>
        </md>
        Setting <m>f_x = 0</m> gives
        <m>3(x+y)^2 = 3 \Rightarrow (x+y)^2 = 1 \Rightarrow x+y = 1</m> or
        <m>x+y=-1</m>. In either case <m>(x+y)^2 = 1</m>, so setting
        <m>f_y=0</m> gives
        <md>
          3(1) + 6y + 6 = 0 \Rightarrow 6y = -9 \Rightarrow y = -\frac{3}{2}
        </md>
        If <m>x+y=1</m> then
        <m>x = 1-\left(-\frac{3}{2}\right) = \frac{5}{2}</m>, and if
        <m>x+y=-1</m> then
        <m>x = -1-\left(-\frac{3}{2}\right) = \frac{1}{2}</m>. Hence the
        critical points are <m>\left(\frac{5}{2},-\frac{3}{2}\right)</m> and
        <m>\left(\frac{1}{2},-\frac{3}{2}\right)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        To classify them we use the Second Derivative Test. The second
        partial derivatives are
        <md>
          f_{xx} = 6x+6y = 6(x+y), \quad f_{yy} = 6x+6y+6 = 6(x+y)+6, \quad f_{xy} = 6x+6y = 6(x+y)
        </md>
        so the discriminant is
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>D(x,y) = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = 6(x+y)\left[ 6(x+y)+6 \right] - \left[ 6(x+y) \right]^2</mrow>
          <mrow>= 36(x+y)^2 + 36(x+y) - 36(x+y)^2 = 36(x+y)</mrow>
        </md>
        At <m>\left(\frac{5}{2},-\frac{3}{2}\right)</m> we have <m>x+y=1</m>,
        so <m>D = 36 \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx} = 6 \gt 0</m>; hence <m>f</m>
        has a local minimum at <m>\left(\frac{5}{2},-\frac{3}{2}\right)</m>.
        At <m>\left(\frac{1}{2},-\frac{3}{2}\right)</m> we have
        <m>x+y=-1</m>, so <m>D = -36 \lt 0</m>; hence <m>f</m> has a saddle
        point at <m>\left(\frac{1}{2},-\frac{3}{2}\right)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
